<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dmitry Donskoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1350">1350</a>–<a href="/years/1389">1389</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Dmitry Ivanovich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dmitry Donskoy was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1359 and of Vladimir from 1362. He built the first stone Kremlin in Moscow and made Moscow the leading Russian principality over Tver and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>. His victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a> in 1380, on the Don, gave him his name, though Moscow was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> in 1382.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy.jpg" alt="Dmitry Donskoy" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown. Died over 100 years ago · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitri_Donskoy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dmitry Ivanovich was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 12 October 1350, the eldest son of Ivan II of Moscow and his second wife, Alexandra Velyaminova, daughter of Vasily Velyaminov, the tysyatsky (military commander) of Moscow. Ivan died on 13 November 1359, when Dmitry was nine. Real power then rested with his father's advisers, led by Velyaminov, while Metropolitan Alexius supervised the boy's education and wielded significant influence in the government.</p><p>In 1360, amid political turmoil in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the khan Nawruz Beg gave the Grand Principality of Vladimir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-of-suzdal">Dmitry Konstantinovich of Suzdal</a>. In 1362 Dmitry Ivanovich received a patent (yarlyk) from the rival khan Murad and, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> boyars and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-bold">Vladimir Andreyevich of Serpukhov</a>, drove his rival from Vladimir. In January 1366 he married the rival's youngest daughter, Eudoxia, which secured peace between the two principalities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After taking the throne of Tver in 1366, Mikhail Aleksandrovich challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> with Lithuanian support. A Lithuanian army came to his aid, but the new stone walls of Moscow withstood the siege. In 1370 Dmitry invaded Tver again, and the Lithuanian grand duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a>, finding him well prepared, proposed a truce and withdrew. Mikhail received a patent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> in 1371 but could not enforce it, and a treaty negotiated by Alexius had Algirdas recognise the grand principality as Dmitry's patrimony. On 1 September 1375 Mikhail acknowledged Dmitry as his elder brother.</p><p>From 1374 Dmitry refused to pay tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a>. In 1378 his forces defeated a Mongol army at the Battle of Vozha River. In 1380 Mamai himself led a large force against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Sergius of Radonezh blessed Dmitry before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a>, and the battle opened with a single combat in which the Russian champion Alexander Peresvet and Temir-Murza of the Horde killed each other. Dmitry won, and from the battle took his name Donskoy, 'of the Don'. Tokhtamysh then deposed Mamai and overran Moscow in 1382; Dmitry pledged loyalty to him and was reinstated as grand prince of Vladimir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dmitry died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 19 May 1389. He was the first grand prince to bequeath his titles to his son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-i-of-moscow">Vasily I of Moscow</a>, without consulting the khan. He had at least twelve children with Eudoxia. In Russian historiography he is regarded as a national hero and a central figure of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> canonised him as a saint on 6 June 1988, with his feast day on 19 May.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dmitry Donskoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's weakness showed during the Great Troubles: in 1380 the warlord Mamai was defeated by Grand Prince <b>Dmitry of Moscow</b> at the Battle of Kulikovo, although Khan Tokhtamysh sacked Moscow two years later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> replaced the oak walls of the Kremlin with white limestone in 1367–1368, and the new fortress withstood sieges by Algirdas of Lithuania in 1368 and 1370.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the fourteenth century Moscow’s princes, holding the title of grand prince of Vladimir, claimed the right to be Novgorod’s prince and demanded tribute; <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> forced the city to pay a large indemnity in 1386.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Dmitry Konstantinovich held the grand-princely title of Vladimir from 1360 to 1363 before giving way to <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b>, who married his daughter Evdokia.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-nizhny-novgorod-suzdal">Grand Principality of Nizhny Novgorod-Suzdal</a></li><li><blockquote>Oleg Ivanovich, who ruled for most of 1350 to 1402, stood up to <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> and kept out of the Battle of Kulikovo.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-ryazan">Grand Principality of Ryazan</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Fyodor Romanovich and his son were killed fighting for <b>Dmitry Donskoy</b> at Kulikovo in 1380.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-beloozero">Principality of Beloozero</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1375" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dmitry Donskoy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1375">What was here in 1375? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
